In article <12054@pasteur.Berkeley.EDU> kemnitz@eden.Berkeley.EDU (Greg Kemnitz) writes:
>the following:  have a simple helm of alignment.  The alignment you get by
>wearing it should depend on its cursed/blessed state, ie
>
>blessed : you become lawful
>normal : you become neutral
>cursed : you become chaotic
>
>Also, the helm should not be identified unless you actually change alignment
>by wearing it, ie a chaotic character wearing a cursed helm would not identify
>it until it was uncursed.  This scheme seems to me to be more consistent with
>how altars work (for blessing/cursing/neutralizing water potions) and gets rid
>of the notion of "oppositeness" for neutral characters being chaotic.  

There are two problems with this:

1) There is no connection between blessed and lawful, cursed and
chaotic.  In the game there is nothing "good" about lawful, or "bad"
about chaotic.  You just can't draw that kind of parallel.  It would
make just as much sense to have cursed helms be lawful, uncursed helms
chaotic, and blessed helms neutral.

2) Remember that you can't take off a cursed helmet.  Under your
scheme, a chaotic character with such a helmet would be able to change
alignments at will, while neutral and lawful characters would only be
able to change between lawful and neutral without fear of the change
becoming permanent.  If they were to get a helm of alignment and
wanted to use it to change between chaotic and their alignment, they
wouldn't be able to because taking off the helm would require
converting it to neutral.

This is just a spur of the moment thought, but...

Maybe it would be better to have helms of alignment intrinsically
change the wearer to a specific alignment, i.e. a chaotic helm of
alignment would change anybody of any alignment to chaotic, etc.
Maybe converting a helm of alignment under that scheme could be done
by attempting to sacrifice it on an altar of the target alignment.

Anyway, I'd like to hear what people think...

